window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"11-02","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"11-02-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-01/","title_he":"א – ערך הנישואין","title_en":"1. The Value of Marriage","body_en":"<p>One cannot estimate how great is the value of the mitzva of marriage, which gives expression to all the values together: love and holiness, unity and faith, faithfulness and joy, life and blessing. Two great mitzvot are fulfilled through marriage. The first: marriage itself, in which each of the spouses undertakes to love and care for the good and well-being of the other and to gladden the other as much as possible. The second: 'be fruitful and multiply'.</p>\n<p>Let us first explain the value of marriage: the complete person is the person who lives in love and joy within the framework of marriage, for the man is not complete without his wife, and the woman is not complete without her husband. And so great is the virtue of marriage that the Sages said (Yevamot 63a): \"Any man who has no wife is not a man.\" They further said (ibid. 62b): any man who has no wife lives without joy, without blessing, without goodness, without Torah, without a wall, without peace. And of course every woman who has no husband also lacks all these virtues.</p>\n<p>Within the framework of marriage a person can fulfil completely the mitzva \"You shall love your fellow as yourself\" (Vayikra 19:18), of which Rabbi Akiva said that it is \"a great principle of the Torah\" (Sifra ibid.). For only between spouses is love fulfilled completely in all areas of life, spiritual and physical. It follows that when the spouses live together in love, each of them loving his or her spouse no less than he loves himself, and wanting to gladden the other with an extra joy no less than he wants to rejoice himself, they fulfil in a concentrated way the whole Torah (the Arizal, Sefer Ha-Likkutim, Ekev). The peak of joy and love is expressed in the mitzva of ona (marital intimacy), and the neglect of this mitzva is the main ground for divorce, as explained in tractate Ketubot (63b). The principles and details of the mitzva of ona are explained in Peninei Halakha, 'Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato' (1:1-3; 2:1-5).</p>\n<p>Through marriage that is kept in faithfulness, love and joy, a supernal light flows onto the loving spouses and adds life and blessing to the world. And as Rabbi Akiva expounded (Sota 17a): \"Man and woman – if they are worthy, the Shekhina is between them; if they are not worthy, fire consumes them.\" In the word for man, <i>ish</i>, the letter <i>yod</i> appears, and in the word for woman, <i>isha</i>, the letter <i>heh</i> appears; together they form the Name <i>Y-ah</i>, which is one of the seven holy Names of God.</p>\n<p>Since the love and joy between man and woman are so important, the inclination in this too is especially great, and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave man free choice. When he turns this inclination to the good – when the man strives to gladden his wife with an extra joy, and the woman strives to gladden her husband with an extra joy – there is nothing better than it; and when a person turns this inclination to evil, in licentiousness, harlotry and adultery, there is nothing worse than it (PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 3:1-2).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-02/","title_he":"ב – האהבה והאחדות","title_en":"2. Love and Unity","body_en":"<p>Many think mistakenly that marriage is intended chiefly in order to fulfil the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply', but the truth is that the value of marriage is greater and loftier. To understand this one must return to the foundations of faith: the Holy One, blessed be He, created the world lacking, so that human beings could repair it and make it good and happy, and thereby merit to be partners with the Holy One, blessed be He, in all the good in the world, and their joy in it would be complete. Separation is the deepest lack that exists in creation. Admittedly, the One God created all the creatures, but since He hid His light, the creatures became separate from Him, may He be blessed, and consequently also became separate from one another, each one caring for himself. From this stem all the conflicts, the disputes, the quarrels and the wars. The main aim of the faith of Israel is to pass through the screens, to believe in the One God and to reveal the unity in the world. This world is called 'alma de-feruda' and 'alma de-shikra' – the world of separation and falsehood, in which the root of unity is not recognised, and all the evils in the world stem from this. By contrast, the essence of the faith of Israel is the faith of unity, faith in the One God, and thereby we repair the world.</p>\n<p>This is also the reason for the great importance of the mitzva of settling the Land, which joins heaven and earth; for the most fundamental separation is the separation between heaven and earth, which is expressed in the separation between spirit and matter, between vision and reality, between the Creator and creation. Through the mitzva of settling the Land it is revealed that Hashem is God in heaven and on earth, and all earthly matters are connected to holiness. Therefore the Sages said (Ketubot 110b): \"Whoever dwells in the Land of Israel is like one who has a God, and whoever dwells outside the Land is like one who has no God… and as if he worships idols\" (see below 3:15). The value of unity is also the foundation of the special importance of the mitzva \"You shall love your fellow as yourself\" (Vayikra 19:18), of which Rabbi Akiva said that it is \"a great principle of the Torah\" (Sifra ibid.).</p>\n<p>Now one can understand how great is the virtue of the mitzva of love and joy between a man and his wife, through which the mitzva \"You shall love your fellow as yourself\" is fulfilled in the complete way. It expresses the greatest unity, for it unites completely two separate and different people. This unity is doubled and redoubled: a unity of a man and his wife, and a unity of the soul and the body. Many times there is a contradiction between the soul and the body: the soul yearns for spiritual pleasures and through them for the good, and the body is drawn to bodily pleasures and through them to evil; the soul desires eternity and the body the passing present. In this mitzva the soul and the body fuse together, and even the evil inclination is turned to the good. Through this mitzva the noble idea of faithfulness and unity is joined with the greatest physical pleasure. The moral value of total devotion is joined with the greatest joy, and the passing bodily joy is united with eternal existence for generations (PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 1:1-8).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-03/","title_he":"ג – מעלת מצוות פרו ורבו","title_en":"3. The Virtue of the Mitzva of Being Fruitful and Multiplying","body_en":"<p>The second mitzva that is fulfilled through marriage is the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply'. And since life is the basic aim of creation, this is the first mitzva mentioned in the Torah. As was said to Adam and Ḥava at the conclusion of the work of creation (Bereshit 1:28): \"And God blessed them, and God said to them: Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth and subdue it.\" It is further said in Parashat Noaḥ after the Flood (9:1): \"And God blessed Noaḥ and his sons, and said to them: Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth.\" And after He warned against murder, He added (ibid. 7): \"And you, be fruitful and multiply; swarm in the earth and multiply in it.\"</p>\n\n<p>Through this mitzva a person walks in the ways of Hashem: just as Hashem created the world and sustains it, so a person begets children, cares for them and raises them. And thereby he becomes a partner with the Holy One, blessed be He, as the Sages said (Nidda 31a): \"There are three partners in a person: the Holy One, blessed be He, his father and his mother.\"</p>\n<p>This is the first and fundamental aim of creation, as the Sages said in the Mishna (Gittin 41b): \"The world was created only for being fruitful and multiplying, as it is said (Yeshayahu 45:18): He did not create it a waste; He formed it to be inhabited.\" The Sages further said in the Mishna (Sanhedrin 37a): \"Whoever sustains one soul of Israel, Scripture regards him as if he had sustained a whole world.\" And if they said this of one who sustains a poor person so that he not die of hunger (Bava Batra 11a), how much more so is the virtue of parents great, who beget a child and raise and educate him, for they truly sustain a whole world.</p>\n<p>Rabbi Eliezer said (Yevamot 63b): \"Whoever does not engage in being fruitful and multiplying is as if he sheds blood,\" as it is said (Bereshit 9:6-7): \"Whoever sheds the blood of man, by man shall his blood be shed…\" and in the verse after it it is said: \"And you, be fruitful and multiply; swarm in the earth and multiply in it.\" The role of a person to beget children is so piercing and fundamental that one who does not fulfil it is considered like one who killed his children before they were born. Rabbi Yaakov said (ibid.): \"Whoever does not engage in being fruitful and multiplying is as if he diminishes the Image,\" as it is said (Bereshit 9:6-7): \"For in the image of God He made man,\" and in the verse after it it is said: \"And you, be fruitful and multiply; swarm in the earth and multiply in it.\" Every person has a uniqueness of his own, and therefore every person reveals a further aspect of the image of God; and one who refrains from engaging in being fruitful and multiplying diminishes the appearance of the Divine revelation in the world.</p>\n<p>In the generations after the Holocaust, the importance of this mitzva has grown very greatly. Our enemies, may their names be blotted out, murdered a third of our people, and we who remain must make up the lack, set up a memorial to them, and strive that the blessing be fulfilled in us: the more they afflict us, the more we will multiply and the more we will spread out.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-04/","title_he":"ד – חזקיהו","title_en":"4. Ḥizkiyahu","body_en":"<p>To understand the importance of the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' it is fitting to recall the story of King Ḥizkiyahu, from which we learn that whoever of his own free will does not fulfil this mitzva, even if he is a great tzaddik, has no share in the World to Come. At the time when the mighty army of Sanḥeriv king of Ashur was besieging Jerusalem to destroy it, King Ḥizkiyahu fell ill, as it is said (Yeshayahu 38:1): \"In those days Ḥizkiyahu was sick unto death. And Yeshayahu son of Amotz the prophet came to him and said to him: Thus says the Lord: Set your house in order, for you shall die and not live.\" Already years before, Ḥizkiyahu had known that a great danger faced him from the kingdom of Ashur; for, following the sins that had increased in Israel, the kingdom of Ashur had already subdued the kingdom of Israel in Shomron and exiled the ten tribes from the Land (II Melakhim 17), and the threat to the kingdom of Yehuda was near and tangible. To prevent the evil, Ḥizkiyahu commanded the whole people to repent and to strengthen themselves in Torah. \"He stuck a sword at the entrance of the study hall and said: Whoever does not engage in Torah shall be pierced with this sword. They searched from Dan to Be'er Sheva and found no ignoramus; from Gevat to Antipatris and found no boy or girl, man or woman, who was not expert in the laws of impurity and purity\" (Sanhedrin 94b).</p>\n<p>And behold, in his hard hour, when the disaster he had feared was about to happen and the army of Ashur was already besieging Jerusalem, and he himself had fallen sick, the prophet came to him with a terrible announcement: \"Set your house in order, for you shall die and not live – 'you shall die' in this world, 'and not live' in the World to Come.\" Ḥizkiyahu cried out with the question: in what have I sinned, that it has been decreed upon me to be cut off from this world and from the World to Come?! The prophet answered: \"Because you did not engage in being fruitful and multiplying.\" Ḥizkiyahu answered: but it was made known to me through the holy spirit that sons who are not worthy will be born to me?! The prophet said to him: \"Why do you involve yourself with the secrets of the Merciful One? What you were commanded you should have done, and what is pleasing before the Holy One, blessed be He, let Him do.\" That is: what business have you calculating the hidden things of the Holy One, blessed be He!? What you were commanded – you must do, and what is good in the eyes of the Holy One, blessed be He – He will do. Ḥizkiyahu understood that he had sinned, and asked Yeshayahu to give him his daughter as a wife, so that perhaps his merit and Yeshayahu's merit would help that good sons be born to him. The prophet answered him: the decree has already been sealed upon you. The king said to him: \"Son of Amotz, finish your prophecy and go out! So I have received from the house of my father's father (King David): even if a sharp sword is resting on a person's neck, he should not refrain from asking for mercy\" (Berakhot 10a).</p>\n<p>\"Then Ḥizkiyahu turned his face to the wall and prayed to the Lord, and said: Please, O Lord, remember now how I have walked before You in truth and with a whole heart, and have done what is good in Your eyes; and Ḥizkiyahu wept greatly\" (Yeshayahu 38:2-3). And Hashem heard his voice and commanded Yeshayahu to tell Ḥizkiyahu that He had added fifteen more years to his years, and would also save him from the army of Ashur. That night the angel of Hashem went out and struck the army of Sanḥeriv, and Jerusalem was saved; and Ḥizkiyahu married the daughter of Yeshayahu the prophet, and Menashe was born to them. Indeed Ḥizkiyahu's fear was borne out: Menashe his son, who reigned after him, did evil in the eyes of Hashem, worshipped idols and also shed very much innocent blood, until the decree of the destruction of the First Temple was sealed (II Melakhim 19-21). And nevertheless, the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' did not move from its place, for it is the foundation of the existence of the world. And even in the case of Ḥizkiyahu, through his son Menashe the wicked, the dynasty of the house of David continued, from which Mashiaḥ son of David will be born, who will come speedily in our days.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-05/","title_he":"ה – גדרי מצוות פריה ורביה","title_en":"5. The Definitions of the Mitzva of Being Fruitful and Multiplying","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' belongs to the man and the woman alike, and in a certain respect the woman's reward is greater, for according to the pain is the reward (Avot 5:23). But regarding the obligation they disagreed. In the view of the Sages, the man is obligated in the mitzva, because he is the one who betroths in marriage, and the obligation is on him to initiate the union in the mitzva of ona. And to this the verse alluded: \"Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth and subdue it\" – \"it is the way of a man to subdue, and it is not the way of a woman to subdue\" (Yevamot 65b). Some explained that since the woman has pain and danger in pregnancy and childbirth, the Torah, whose ways are ways of pleasantness, did not want to impose this mitzva on the woman as an obligation (Meshekh Ḥokhma, Bereshit 9:7). On the other hand, in the view of Rabbi Yoḥanan ben Beroka the woman too is obligated in the mitzva, for the mitzva was said in the plural, to Adam and Ḥava, as it is said (Bereshit 1:28): \"And God blessed them, and God said to them: Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth and subdue it.\"</p>\n<p>In practice it was ruled that the obligation of the mitzva is on the man. But this does not harm the woman's right: if it became clear that she is married to a sterile man, and she wanted to divorce in order to merit a son or a daughter who could help her in her old age, her husband is compelled to divorce her and give her her ketuba (Yevamot 65b; SA EH 1:13; 154:6; PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 5, n. 2).</p>\n<p>As in the mitzvot of Torah study and prayer, so in this mitzva too we find that the Torah addresses the men in the language of obligation and the women in the language of a voluntary mitzva, and thereby the mitzva is fulfilled in the complete way from both its sides, as obligation and as free offering together. The practical difference is that a woman who wanted not to marry, or who wanted to marry one who cannot have children, although she excludes herself from a very great mitzva, is not considered a transgressor, since there is no obligation on her to fulfil the mitzva. But a man may not remain single, and may not marry a barren woman, before he has fulfilled the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying (PH ibid. 5:8).</p>\n<p>As we have learned, with a son and a daughter one fulfils the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' from the Torah. One who had a son and a daughter and they died in his lifetime: if each of them managed to beget one child, although they died in their father's lifetime, their father has fulfilled the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying, for his seed continues through his two children. And if one of them died in his father's lifetime without having managed to beget children, the mitzva has lapsed from the father, because he did not leave after him a son and a daughter who can continue to fulfil the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying. And likewise if his son or his daughter is sterile, since they cannot beget, their father too has not fulfilled the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying. And even if his son begot twelve children, if his daughter is barren, since his seed did not continue from his son and his daughter, he has not fulfilled the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying (SA EH 1:5).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-06/","title_he":"ו – מצווה להרבות ילדים","title_en":"6. A Mitzva to Have Many Children","body_en":"<p>Although with a son and a daughter one fulfils the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' from the Torah, the Sages established as a binding mitzva to add and bear more children (Yevamot 62b), because we have learned in the Torah that such is the will of the Creator, as it is said (Bereshit 1:28): \"And God said to them: Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth…\" And likewise Hashem blessed the fathers of the nation in a special way, as was said to Avraham Avinu: \"That I will surely bless you, and I will greatly multiply your seed\" (ibid. 22:17), and to Yitzḥak Avinu: \"And I will multiply your seed as the stars of the heavens\" (ibid. 26:4), and to Yaakov Avinu: \"And your seed shall be as the dust of the earth\" (ibid. 28:14). And so Hashem promised Israel if they keep the Torah and the mitzvot: \"And I will make you fruitful and multiply you\" (Vayikra 26:9). And so in Moshe's blessing to Israel: \"May the Lord, the God of your fathers, add to you a thousand times as many as you are\" (Devarim 1:11). And likewise of the redemption it is said: \"And they shall be fruitful and multiply\" (Yirmiyahu 23:3), \"And I will multiply man and beast upon you, and they shall multiply and be fruitful\" (Yeḥezkel 36:11), \"I will multiply them with men like a flock\" (ibid. 37). And so wrote the Rambam (Hilkhot Ishut 15:16): \"Even though a person has fulfilled the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying (with a son and a daughter), he is commanded by the words of the Scribes not to cease from being fruitful and multiplying as long as he has strength, for whoever adds one soul to Israel is as if he built a world.\"</p>\n<p>A further reason for the Sages' mitzva to add sons and daughters is that even after a person has begotten a son and a daughter, it is not clear that both of them will merit to raise descendants, and consequently it is not clear that he will succeed in fulfilling the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' from the Torah. And this is what Rabbi Yehoshua said: \"If he had sons in his youth, let him have sons in his old age,\" as it is said (Kohelet 11:6): \"In the morning sow your seed, and in the evening do not withhold your hand; for you do not know which will prosper, whether this or that, or whether both alike will be good\" (Yevamot 62b).</p>\n<p>Seemingly one could say that the obligation the Sages established has no limit, but every person must strive with all his strength to beget as many children as possible. But it seems more likely that there are two levels in the Sages' mitzva: the first, an obligation equal for everyone, of about four to five children; the second, a mitzva to add more children, each couple according to its strength. The basis of this division is that we find that the Sages established their mitzvot on the model of the mitzva from the Torah; and since the Torah established as an obligation to beget two children, the obligation from the words of the Sages is to add another two children. And perhaps one may even say that the mitzva is that there be two sons and two daughters, and then in most cases they will need to have five children for this. And so we find that it is accepted in most religious families, who strive to have four to five children (PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 5:6, n. 6).</p>\n<p>According to this, ordinary parents, who are not especially ill in body or soul, have a mitzva to fulfil the Sages' mitzva of having four or five children. After that, they should consider whether they have the strength to continue fulfilling the great mitzva and to beget more children. For example, if they know that they will be able to raise more children and educate them in Torah, mitzvot and proper conduct, it is a mitzva that they continue to bear children as far as they are able. But if they know that with more children the burden on them will be too heavy, and anger and nervousness will accompany their lives, there is room for them not to bear more; for although with every additional child they will fulfil a mitzva, on the other hand, in their poor mental state they will commit transgressions, and the matter may have a bad influence on the education of the children. Also those who want to direct their strengths to other worthwhile channels, in a way that will not leave them strength to raise more children, may do so.</p>\n<p>It follows that although with every single child the spouses beget they fulfil a great mitzva from the Torah, there are three levels in the definitions of the obligation of the mitzva:<br><b>a)</b> An obligation from the Torah to beget a son and a daughter; and even when conditions are hard, one must make great efforts to fulfil it (PH ibid. 5:13-15; 6:1-4). <b>b)</b> An obligation from the words of the Sages to make efforts to bear about four or five children.<br><b>c)</b> A mitzva to continue to beget more children according to the parents' strength. These laws were explained at length in Peninei Halakha, 'Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato'. (In chapter 5:17-19 it is explained which contraceptives are permitted, and there, 13-16, the details of the laws and the considerations for postponing pregnancy, and in halakha 20, when it is right to ask a Torah scholar.)</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-07/","title_he":"ז – בני זוג שאינם זוכים לילדים","title_en":"7. Couples Who Do Not Merit Children","body_en":"<p>Very hard are the sufferings of a couple who wait to be remembered and do not merit it. Their friends who married after them are already embracing children, and they are still waiting and hoping. Despite the terrible difficulty, they are commanded to strengthen themselves in faith in Hashem, who leads the world and directs everything for the good. And they must strive greatly to fulfil the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' and to make use of all the tools that medicine provides, and together with this to pray much to the One in whose hand is the soul of every living thing, that He remember them in mercy and grant them the fruit of the womb.<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>In general one should know that sometimes sufferings are intended to arouse them to repentance and to refine their character traits, in order to prepare them to be better parents. Sometimes their sufferings stem from the general lack that exists in the world. Sometimes it is possible through prayers and medical treatments to solve the problem, and sometimes the decree is sealed. In any case, the suffering couple must believe that their sufferings are intended for the good, in order to repair and refine them and the world, and to increase their happiness in this world and in the World to Come.</p>\n<p>In addition, it is fitting to know that when it concerns precious souls, there is difficulty in bringing them down to the world, and only through prayers and the parents' strengthening themselves in Torah and acts of kindness does the world become ready for them. Therefore most of the fathers and mothers of Israel suffered from barrenness. And so we find with Ḥana, who was very distressed by her barrenness, and in the merit of her prayer the heavens opened and Shmuel was born to her, who was the greatest prophet after Moshe Rabbeinu.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, until they are remembered in mercy, a great test stands before them: will they sink into their sorrow and lose their faith and their joy, or will they overcome their sorrow and increase love between them, and fulfil the mitzva of ona with extra joy, and always think how to add goodness and joy to all the members of their family and their friends. For truly, although they did not merit children, there is in their partnership a very great value of its own.</p>\n<p>The masters of the hidden wisdom explained that from every single union made for the sake of the mitzva of ona, that is, in faithfulness, love and desire, an abundance of life and blessing is added to the world, and new souls are created in the upper worlds which are afterwards born through other people. And so it was in all the years that Avraham Avinu and Sara Imeinu were barren: from all the unions between them souls were born, which descended and were born through other people, who afterwards were aroused to draw near to them and to believe in Hashem; and of them it is said (Bereshit 12:5): \"the souls that they had made in Ḥaran.\"</p>\n<p>There is a special purity in the love of those who did not merit children, for it is a love that does not depend on anything and does not rest on the children born of them, but is founded entirely on the very love between them, in which there is an expression of the Divine unity that is revealed in the world; therefore it adds vitality and existence to all the worlds. And so the holy Arizal explained, that there are two kinds of union: one for the purpose of the birth of souls, and the second for the purpose of the vitality and existence of the worlds. And admittedly, couples who merited children also merit to fulfil the second union, for the purpose of the vitality and existence of the worlds, when the woman is pregnant or nursing or when she is older. But since for couples who did not merit children this is their only kind of union, their influence in it is greater. But this is on condition that they indeed merit, through the love and joy between them, to break through the barrier of sadness, to see the world in a good light, to rejoice in the joy of the members of their family and their friends, to engage in the settling of the world and to do kindness with people as far as they are able.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"By the kindness of Hashem, thanks to the development of medicine, today it is possible to solve almost all problems of infertility. Therefore, a couple for whom it has become clear through reliable tests that naturally the chance that they will merit a child is very low are obligated to do everything accepted in medicine in order to fulfil the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' with a son and a daughter. This includes in vitro fertilisation, which is carried out by taking eggs from the woman and sperm from the man and placing them together in a test tube, so that there they will join and begin to develop into an embryo, which is afterwards placed in the womb. And it seems that also treatments that are not included in the ordinary health insurance, if they are treatments that most people who want children are accustomed to undergo, even if their price is high, it is an obligation to undergo them in order to fulfil the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' (PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 6:1)."}]},{"slug":"11-02-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-08/","title_he":"ח – לא ילדו עשר שנים","title_en":"8. They Did Not Have Children for Ten Years","body_en":"<p>If they were married for ten years and did not have children, the Sages assessed that they will no longer merit to beget together, and therefore established that the man is obligated to divorce his wife and pay her her ketuba, and to marry another woman, in order to fulfil the obligation of the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply'. And although divorce is like destruction, and the Sages said (Gittin 90b): \"Whoever divorces his first wife, even the altar sheds tears over him,\" the obligation of the mitzva comes first, for through it a person's seed continues after him. And usually the present sorrow over the divorce is smaller than the long-term sorrow from the lack of children.</p>\n<p>If the man thinks that he is sterile, and that is why they did not merit the fruit of the womb, he need not divorce his wife. But if his wife wants to divorce him in order to marry another man and merit a child, he will be obligated to divorce her and pay her her ketuba. And if she wishes, she can remain with him, because the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' was imposed on her as a mitzva and not as an obligation (Yevamot 64a; SA EH 154:6).</p>\n<p>If during those ten years in which they were not remembered the man or the woman was ill, or they were separated because of a prolonged journey, the times of illness and separation are not counted in the ten years. If the woman became pregnant and miscarried, one counts ten years from the time of the miscarriage (SA EH 154:10-12).</p>\n<p>If they lived abroad and made aliya to the Land, one begins to count ten years from the time they made aliya, for perhaps the merit of the Land will help them. And likewise a couple who lived in the Land and went out for a certain period: the time they were abroad is not counted for them (Rashi and Ramban on Bereshit 16:3; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 154:25).</p>\n<p>All that we have learned, that after ten years one must divorce, is when there is no clear medical opinion. But if before ten years have passed reliable doctors reached the conclusion that there is no chance that the man or the woman will have children, although ten years have not passed, the side that can have children can demand a divorce. And likewise the reverse: if after ten years have passed reliable doctors think that there is still a reasonable chance that they will have children, neither of them can demand a divorce (PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 6:7).</p>\n<p>This obligation that the Sages imposed on a couple who did not merit children for ten years to divorce may be very painful. And there were people, among them Torah scholars, who despite their desire to fulfil the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' found it very hard to part from their beloved wives, with whom they had made an eternal covenant, and sought for themselves a permission to continue to keep the covenant of their marriage although they knew that they would not have children. Indeed, on the basis of several arguments it is sometimes possible to permit this.</p>\n<p>The simple permission is when there is a chance that the problem of infertility exists in both spouses. But even when it is clear that the man is not sterile, if the divorce is very hard for him, to the point that it is hard for him to see his life continuing without his wife, there is room to say that in such a situation he is not obligated to divorce. For the obligation to divorce is in an ordinary situation, but for one for whom the pain of divorce is terrible and dreadful in his eyes, above and beyond the usual, there is no obligation to divorce (the details of this halakha were explained in PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 6:7-8).</p>\n<p>If they adopt a child, they fulfil thereby a great mitzva, for the Sages said: \"Whoever raises an orphan boy or girl in his home, Scripture regards him as if he had begotten him\" (Megilla 13a). Also teachers, men and women, who invest themselves in educating their pupils in Torah, mitzvot and proper conduct are considered partners in their birth, for pupils too are called sons (Sanhedrin 19b). Also a financial contribution to the education of children in distress is a great mitzva and can be considered a certain substitute for the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply'.</p>\n<p>From here we will go on to clarify the fitting age of marriage for men and women, for delaying the age of marriage is one of the main causes of the problem of infertility; for problems that can be solved relatively easily in the twenties are harder to solve in the thirties. Therefore fulfilling the mitzva of marriage at its time prevents many medical and emotional problems.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-09/","title_he":"ט – גיל הנישואין לגברים בעבר","title_en":"9. The Age of Marriage for Men in the Past","body_en":"<p>The question of the age of marriage is complex and complicated. Seemingly, from the time a person becomes obligated in mitzvot, at the age of thirteen, he must fulfil all the mitzvot, including the mitzva of marriage. But on the other hand, the mitzva of marriage involves great responsibility for building the partnership in love and joy, for livelihood and raising children. For this the groom must prepare in two main areas: Torah study and planning a livelihood. Let us explain further.</p>\n<p>The first, the study of the foundations of the Torah, so that it will shape his outlook and he will know how to conduct himself according to halakha. As the Sages said (Mishna Avot 5:21): \"At five years old for Scripture, at ten for Mishna, at thirteen for mitzvot, at fifteen for Talmud, at eighteen for the wedding canopy.\" And so it was ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (YD 246:2): \"A person should study Torah and afterwards marry a wife, for if he marries a wife first, he cannot engage in Torah, since a millstone is on his neck.\" The second, livelihood. In those years in which they studied the foundations of the Torah, they devoted part of the day to work, in order to help the father and in the course of it to learn the craft from which they would be able to earn a living (Sota 44a). And so wrote the Rambam (Hilkhot De'ot 5:11): \"The way of people of understanding is that a person first establishes for himself an occupation that will support him, and afterwards buys a dwelling, and afterwards marries a wife… But fools begin by marrying a wife, and afterwards if they can afford it they buy a house, and afterwards at the end of their days they go back to seek a craft or are supported by charity…\"</p>\n<p>Therefore the Sages instructed to postpone the marriage of men to the age of eighteen, and warned that in any case they should not postpone it beyond the age of twenty. The Sages said (Kiddushin 29b): \"Until twenty years the Holy One, blessed be He, sits and waits for a person, when he will marry a wife. Once he has reached twenty and has not married, He says: may his bones rot\" – a language of curse, for his not fulfilling the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply'. And likewise the Sages said (Kohelet Rabba 3:3): \"A time to give birth and a time to die: from when a person is born, the Holy One, blessed be He, waits for him to marry a wife until twenty years. If he reached twenty and did not marry a wife, the Holy One, blessed be He, says to him: it was a time to give birth for you and you did not want it; this is nothing but a time to die.\"</p>\n<p>In addition the Sages said: \"At twenty years old and he has not married a wife – all his days are in thoughts of transgression,\" because when he draws out the time of his bachelorhood beyond the usual, he cannot preserve the powers of his love for his wife, and he surrenders to his evil inclination in thoughts of transgression. And even after he marries and is faithful to his wife, he will find it hard to be saved from thoughts of transgression, for they have already become part of him. And only if he repents with a great repentance out of love will he be able to repair himself (see PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 5:7; 4:2, n. 2).</p>\n<p>And so it was ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (EH 1:3): \"It is a mitzva for every man to marry a wife at eighteen… and in no case should he pass twenty years without a wife. And one who has passed twenty years and does not want to marry, the court compels him to marry, in order to fulfil the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying.\" Seemingly one should ask: how is it possible to compel fulfilment of this mitzva? After all, marriage must take place in love, and how is it possible to compel a person to marry a woman? Indeed it is clear that they did not compel a person to marry a woman he had not chosen; rather the Sages wanted to express a position of principle, that a person is obligated to marry by the age of twenty, and in principle one can even compel this. In practice, only in rare cases could the court intervene in this. For example, in a case where a young man had a close relationship with a young woman, and they had even agreed to marry, and the young woman already wants to hold the wedding, while the young man wants to but is afraid and delays, and postpones the date of the wedding from time to time – then the court would compel him to marry (PH ibid. 5:8).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-10/","title_he":"י – גיל המצווה למעשה לגברים","title_en":"10. The Age of the Mitzva in Practice for Men","body_en":"<p>Already in the days of the Sages there was a ruling that in a time of pressing need one may postpone marriage until the age of twenty-four (Kiddushin 30a). And so they practised in various periods because of the pressure of livelihood or the spiritual challenges that required longer study before the wedding (Yam Shel Shlomo, Birkei Yosef).</p>\n<p>In recent generations life has become more complex, and the preparation for establishing a family takes more time. In the past, a simple study of Tanakh and musar, of halakha and its reasons, was enough to be ready to establish a Jewish home. It was enough to work with the father a few hours a day in order to acquire by the age of eighteen the professional ability to work and earn a living, and even to save a little money for the needs of the wedding and building the home, which was only one room. But today, in order to cope with the challenges facing a person, one must study much more Torah. For this most young men need to study in a yeshiva framework after the age of eighteen for at least a year, and usually more. A further holy obligation rests on the young men, to defend the people and the Land within the framework of the army, and fulfilling this mitzva too causes marriage to be postponed. Likewise, acquiring a profession that suits a person's talents usually requires studies that last several years and take place after military service. Also the homes we are accustomed to live in are more expensive, because they are larger and equipped with water and electricity systems, and to buy them one must work a number of years.</p>\n<p>If the young people postpone marriage until they finish studying all the foundations of the Torah, finish studying the suitable profession and buy a small apartment, most of them will marry after the age of thirty. Such a postponement is not possible according to halakha, because although the environment in which we live has become more complex, complicated and challenging, a person's emotional and physical nature has not changed, and the time of marriage suited to him is at the age of eighteen to twenty.</p>\n<p>Therefore, in the complex state of our lives it is permitted to postpone marriage beyond the age of twenty, but not later than the age of twenty-four. On the one hand, one must give the young people a few more years, so that they can establish themselves more firmly in Torah and in shaping their outlook, and manage to take the first steps towards acquiring a profession, or at least be able to set out a practical plan for acquiring a profession and supporting the family. And on the other hand, one cannot move further from the time that is suitable, emotionally and physically, for marriage, so as not to lose the enthusiasm and joy of youth that suit the building of the partnership in its first stages. And so we find that those who postpone their marriage beyond the young age find it harder to find their match, and there are those who remain single for many years. Beyond this, the mitzvot of marriage and 'be fruitful and multiply' obligate a person to express himself in a complete way; and as the Sages said, only after a person marries is he called a man (Yevamot 63a), for only then can he truly merit joy, blessing, goodness, Torah, a wall and peace (ibid. 62b), and there is a limit to how many years a person can go on living in so lacking a way. In addition, we have learned that postponing marriage beyond what is necessary causes a person's inclination to overpower him, until all his days he will not be able to free himself of thoughts of transgression (Kiddushin 29b). Therefore one must instruct the public not to postpone marriage beyond the age of twenty-four. And may blessing come to whoever can bring his marriage earlier without significantly harming Torah study, military service and the preparation for acquiring a profession suited to his talent.</p>\n<p>It must be explained that the intention of this halakha is to direct young people to the time at which they should strive to marry; but since marriage also depends on the partner, there will be young people who strive to marry at the proper time and will not succeed. And if they made the proper effort, they bear no sin. Likewise, the instruction not to postpone marriage beyond the age of twenty-four is intended for an ordinary situation, but there are situations in which there is a great need to postpone marriage, and then the matter is sometimes permitted (as explained in PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 5, n. 8).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The age of marriage greatly influences the growth of the family. Over two hundred and ten years the people of Israel grew in Egypt from seventy souls to six hundred thousand men over the age of twenty. If the average age of marriage was 15, it would have been enough for an average woman to bear 6 children, with an average gap of two years between births. But if the average age of marriage was 22, an average woman would have needed to bear 12 children to reach six hundred thousand. A further example: in a family in which the average number of children is six, if the average age of marriage is 21, then when the heads of the family reach the age of seventy-seven they will have 150 descendants; and if their average age of marriage is 25, when they reach the age of seventy-seven they will have 46 descendants. This of course has an aspect for all Israel, in the generation of the Holocaust and the rebirth."}]},{"slug":"11-02-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-11/","title_he":"יא – גיל הנישואין לנשים בעבר","title_en":"11. The Age of Marriage for Women in the Past","body_en":"<p>Although women fulfil a great mitzva in marriage just as the man does, the Sages did not set an age by which it is fitting for women to marry. For all the mitzvot connected with establishing the family and supporting it, and likewise the mitzva of Torah study, were imposed on the men as an obligation and on the women as a mitzva. And since the men are obligated to manage before the wedding to acquire the foundations of the Torah and to prepare for the challenge of livelihood, it was necessary to define for them the time of marriage, so that it would not be postponed excessively. But for women, for whom all this is a mitzva and not an obligation, the Sages did not define an age.</p>\n<p>In practice the custom was that once the young woman was ripe for marriage and agreed and wanted to marry the young man suggested to her, they would begin to plan the wedding. And unlike the young man, who needed to begin working in order to prepare for his wife a house and an orchard from which he would support her (Sota 44a), the woman was not obligated in these preparations towards the wedding. Rather the Torah commanded the parents to help their daughters marry in the best way, and the Sages instructed that the parents should devote about a tenth of their property to help their daughters marry (Ketubot 52b; SA EH 113:1). And consequently the daughters could marry young, around the age of fifteen.</p>\n<p>In periods of want and hardship, many families were forced to marry off their daughters while they were still girls, in order to secure their future, that they not go hungry for bread and merit to establish a family. Therefore the Torah permitted a father to marry off his daughter while she was a minor. Not only this, but although the instruction was to give each daughter a tenth of the property and no more, so as not to deprive the sons and so as to leave the parents property from which they could support themselves in their old age, in situations of hardship the parents were forced to give their daughters much more than a tenth of their property, for without this they would not succeed in arranging a match, and there was a concern that the daughters would remain lonely all the days of their lives. But when there was no existential necessity to marry off the daughters while they were minors, the Sages forbade it and said: \"A person may not betroth his daughter when she is a minor, until she grows up and says: I want so-and-so\" (Kiddushin 41a; SA EH 37:8).</p>\n<p>In recent centuries, when the economic situation improved and stabilised, and there was no longer a need to marry off young girls in order to secure their existence, in most countries the custom of marrying off minor girls was completely abolished. And still in times and places where the hardship was great, as sometimes in Yemen, they were sometimes forced to marry off very young girls (PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 5:10, n. 9).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-12/","title_he":"יב – גיל הנישואין לנשים בזמנינו","title_en":"12. The Age of Marriage for Women in Our Time","body_en":"<p>In recent generations, together with the rise in the economic standard and the possibility for women to express their talents in various fields, the age of marriage of daughters has become later. There are two reasons for this: a) Since daughters can express their talents in many fields, a great mitzva rests on them to study more Torah and to train in the profession suited to them, so that they can add goodness and blessing to the world. b) In the past young couples lived within the framework of an extended family, and even young girls could bear children, with the older women helping them to raise them. But today, when young couples build their lives in a separate framework of their own, the age of marriage is delayed until the time when the daughter is able to bear alone the responsibility for caring for her children.</p>\n<p>In any case, the fitting age of marriage for daughters is a little before that of men. First, because daughters mature earlier, just as the time of their obligation in mitzvot is a year earlier, at the age of twelve. Second, the mitzva of Torah study obligates them in a little less investment, for they are obligated in what is needed for guidance in our complex life in faith, ethics and halakha, whereas the sons are obligated also in the study of foundations of the Torah not directly connected with guidance in life. Third, the obligation of the army rests on the men.</p>\n<p>Admittedly, in the matter of livelihood women today are partners with men. And on the contrary, if she finishes her professional studies earlier, she will be able at first to bear the main burden of livelihood and thereby enable her husband to acquire a fitting profession, so that they can live in comfort, establish a blessed family, give their children a good education and contribute to the settling of the world.</p>\n<p>To sum up, the fitting age of marriage today for men is between twenty and twenty-four, and the fitting age for women is about two years earlier.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-13/","title_he":"יג – האחריות המוטלת על הצעירים","title_en":"13. The Responsibility Resting on Young People","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of marriage sets before young people today a great challenge. Within a few years they must establish their Torah outlook, acquire a profession suited to their talent and establish a family, while the young men are also required to serve in the army and to study more Torah.</p>\n<p>Those first responsible for meeting the challenge are the young people themselves, who must plan their way well, so that they do not lose time in these precious years. For even after we have defined our time as a time of pressing need, in which it is permitted to postpone marriage until the age of twenty-four, and in special cases even more, one who wastes his time in these years neglects the mitzva of the Torah. Therefore every young man and young woman is obligated to pave for themselves the way in which they can combine all the values together: to marry at a young age and at the same time to acquire a profession that suits their talent, to the glory of their family, and to the glory of the Torah, the people and the Land.</p>\n\n<p>To understand how great is the responsibility to fulfil the mitzva at the proper time without delays, it is worthwhile to recall a halakha. As is known, because of the great virtue of the holiness of a Torah scroll, the Sages forbade selling it, and said that one who sells a Torah scroll never sees a sign of blessing (Megilla 27a). And even when the owner of the Torah scroll has nothing to eat except with hardship, he should not sell his Torah scroll. And even when he has no money to buy the needs of a mitzva, such as tefillin and a mezuza, he should not sell his Torah scroll (SA YD 270:1).</p>\n<p>But there are two mitzvot which, because of their great importance, when there is no possibility of fulfilling them without selling the Torah scroll, one sells a Torah scroll and uses the money received in exchange for it to fulfil them. And these are: Torah study and marriage (SA EH 1:2; OḤ 153:6). And even the community may sell a Torah scroll in its possession in order to marry off an orphan boy or girl (SA OḤ 153:6). The special virtue of these two mitzvot is that through them the aim of the Torah is realised: study, through which the words of the Torah become alive in the hearts; and marriage, through which complete life is realised and the children are born who will keep the Torah (Megilla 27a).<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>This law, admittedly, is not common today, because there is almost no situation in which a person cannot marry because of his poverty, for one can always find good Jews who will help him with his basic needs so that he can marry. But we have learned from this law an important principle: they permitted selling a Torah scroll in order to fulfil the mitzva of marriage, and did not say that one should wait a few years until another solution is found. All the more so must young men and young women hasten in the preparations required towards marriage, in Torah study and acquiring a profession, and not waste precious time in these years.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Some say that this permission is only for one who has not yet fulfilled the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' with a son and a daughter, but one who already has a son and a daughter may not sell a Torah scroll for the sake of marriage (SA EH 1). And in the view of many, also one who fulfilled the mitzva with a son and a daughter and was widowed may sell a Torah scroll in order to marry a wife and beget more children. And this is the view of most of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim (Nimukei Yosef, Ritva). And some say that also in order to marry an older woman who can no longer bear children one may sell a Torah scroll, because of the very virtue of marriage and the mitzva of ona, without which a person is not complete (Ramban, Ḥelkat Meḥokek EH 1:10). See PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 5:21, n. 21."}]},{"slug":"11-02-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-14/","title_he":"יד – מצווה על ההורים לחתן את ילדיהם","title_en":"14. Parents Have a Mitzva to Marry Off Their Children","body_en":"<p>The Sages said (Kiddushin 30b) that parents have a mitzva to marry off their children, that is, to help with everything needed for their children's marriage, as it is said (Yirmiyahu 29:6): \"Take wives and beget sons and daughters, and take wives for your sons, and give your daughters to husbands, that they may bear sons and daughters.\" That is, the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply' does not end with the birth of the children, but continues afterwards too, when their children reach the proper age; then the parents must encourage them to marry and help them with advice and financial help, and in this they are active partners in the continuation of the generations. The Sages instructed parents to devote about a tenth of their property for the wedding of each of their daughters. And it is implied that a similar sum would reach the sons through the inheritance. Today the custom is to make the sons and daughters equal in the inheritance, and likewise to make the sons and daughters equal in help for marriage.</p>\n<p>In our time, when most young people choose their partners according to character and the whole personality, and the question \"how much will the parents give?\" determines their ability to marry less, the mitzva resting on the parents to marry off their children is spread over many years: first, in caring to give them a good education and in financing their professional studies, and in educating them and equipping them with fine clothes, and of course in helping to finance the expenses of the wedding and buying furniture.</p>\n<p>In addition, calculating a tenth of the property in our days is also a complex matter, because a significant part of the property is invested in pension savings and invested in an apartment that cannot be realised; likewise one must also take into account the money that the parents are likely to receive in the future from salaries and investment profits. But the principle learned from the mitzva to invest a tenth of the property for the wedding of each daughter is that parents have a mitzva to devote a respectable sum of their property for the marriage of each of their children.</p>\n<p>Since many times children delay marrying because they fear that they will not be able to manage financially, a considerable part of the parents' investment today should be directed towards meeting the great challenge that combines marriage with professional studies, and sometimes also caring for babies. And it seems that also paying for professional studies after the age of eighteen can be considered part of the tenth of the property that parents have a mitzva to invest in the wedding of each of their children, for this too helps in establishing the family and supporting it.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-15/","title_he":"טו – התערבות ההורים בבחירת בני-זוג","title_en":"15. Parents' Involvement in the Choice of Partners","body_en":"<p>The role of the parents is not always easy. On the one hand they must help their children to marry, and on the other hand, the decision itself, whom to marry, is not in their hands. Sometimes hard problems arise around this. The son has chosen his partner, and thinks that her qualities suit his character and his aspirations, but the parents are sure that his choice is mistaken and will bring him into an unhappy marriage. Sometimes the parents are firm in their view, and even threaten to break off ties. And the question is asked: must the son listen to his parents? And likewise the reverse: what should a daughter do whose parents oppose her choice – must she listen to them and give up the choice of her heart?</p>\n<p>As we have learned in the laws of honouring parents (above 1:22), the son or daughter need not listen to their parents in this matter. And although the parents' intention is for the good of their son or daughter, and it is very possible that they are right, the Holy One, blessed be He, gave every person free choice to decide about his future and his life, and the decision whom to marry rests on the one who actually marries. The children are of course obligated to listen to their parents, to hear their position and to weigh it with all seriousness; but if after everything they decided to marry, the parents should not stand in their way and boycott and ostracise them for it. If the parents are sure that the match is not successful and will end in failure, in sorrow and emotional crises, they may decline to take part in financing the wedding, but they have no authority to demand of the son or daughter that they cancel the wedding.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-16/","title_he":"טז – ענייני ממון","title_en":"16. Financial Matters","body_en":"<p>The Sages said (Kiddushin 70a): whoever marries a woman for the sake of money will have sons who are not worthy. For marriage must be based on the love between the spouses and on shared values, and not on an external reason such as money. And if it is done for the sake of money, it does not turn out well, and the sons born of it are not successful. And so wrote the Rema (EH 2:1), that a groom should not quarrel with his bride's family over money, and one who does so does not succeed and his match does not turn out well. For money that a person takes with his wife by way of quarrelling or extortion is not honest money, and one who does so is called one who marries a woman for the sake of money. Rather, whatever his father-in-law and mother-in-law give him he should take with a good eye, and then he will succeed.</p>\n<p>And although we have learned that parents have a mitzva to marry off their children and to invest about a tenth of their property for this, this mitzva is theirs and under their responsibility, and the groom or bride may not compel their parents to do it. Also the court may not compel the parents to help with their son's wedding, even if they are wealthy. Admittedly, for a daughter's wedding, because of the great need in the matter in the days when daughters did not work and earn on their own, the court would compel the daughter's parents to pay for holding a modest wedding, but would not compel them to give according to their wealth (Rema EH 70:1; SA 58:1; Ḥelkat Meḥokek 2).</p>\n<p>To sum up, parents have a mitzva to help with their children's weddings. And if they do not volunteer this of their own accord, the children may ask it of them, but not quarrel. And if they do not give, they should marry for the sake of Heaven, and Hashem will make their way succeed. It is fitting to add words that Rabbi Zilber wrote (Az Nidberu 9:51), which concern chiefly the Ḥaredi public: although parents have a mitzva to help with their daughter's wedding to the value of about a tenth of their property, by law they need not go into debt for this. And also on the children who are about to marry rests the mitzva of honouring father and mother, and they must take care that their parents' investment in their wedding not cause them to sink into large debts beyond their ability, and not take from them the needs of their ongoing living.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-02-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-17/","title_he":"יז – מצוות הכנסת כלה וחובת הציבור","title_en":"17. The Mitzva of Hakhnasat Kalla and the Duty of the Community","body_en":"<p>The community is obligated to help every orphan or poor person to marry. And so it is written in the Shulḥan Arukh (YD 250:1): \"If he (the poor man) has no wife and comes to marry, they marry him off. And they rent a house for him and prepare for him a bed and his household utensils, and afterwards they marry him to a wife.\" It follows that one must take care of the needs of the wedding itself, and likewise that they should have a home and basic furniture. The wedding should include a meal with meat and wine, and the wedding meal should be more honoured than a festival meal. And likewise one should take care that there be musicians or a DJ, for the dancing of rejoicing. And likewise one should take care that the bride and groom have fine clothes.</p>\n<p>And there is no charity more important and greater than marrying off the poor. And if the charity fund has a limited sum that does not allow it to take care of all the needs of the poor, and the charity treasurer faces the question whom to help first, he should put the needs of the wedding of the groom and bride before all other needs (SA YD 249:15). And even when the charity fund is empty, the charity treasurers may not evade this important mitzva; rather they are obligated to borrow money and with it marry off the poor, and afterwards to collect charity in order to repay the debt (SA YD 257:5). If the poor grooms and brides were many, and there is no possibility of borrowing money to marry them all off, one should give precedence to the daughters: one first marries off the poor maidens, and only afterwards, when one can, the poor young men (SA YD 251:8).</p>\n<p>By the kindness of Hashem, in our days the charity treasurers have the possibility of helping every poor groom and bride to hold a modest wedding, buy modest furniture and live in an inexpensive place. But on the other hand, in order to live with dignity today one must acquire a respectable profession, the studies towards which usually last a long time and delay marriage for many. And the mitzva resting today on society and its leaders is to create for the young people the best conditions for fulfilling the mitzva of marriage at its time. For this one must make professional studies as efficient as possible, help young people find housing solutions and dormitories at reasonable prices, and bring forward as much as possible the professional studies of the women, so that in the first years they can help more in supporting the family.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Together with the difficulties that modern life presents, it also has solutions. Admittedly, professional studies last years, but on the other hand the investment in them pays off, and therefore the banks are willing to grant loans for them, whose repayment will come after starting work. And so too regarding homes: they are indeed much larger and more expensive, but an extensive system of loans over decades has also been established that helps in buying them.<br>Beyond the difficulty of combining all the challenges together, the society of plenty has created in many an addiction to luxuries, to the point that many people have become enslaved to them without the ability to realise any ideal. For they are always busy obtaining more money to buy a better car, more expensive clothes and furniture, and a larger house in a more prestigious neighbourhood. For this many people postpone marriage and are lax in the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply'. And one must overcome the inclination and place the ideals above the passing pleasures, and remember that even when a person today makes do with little, he lives as the wealthy lived two hundred years ago. And if then they could live so in happiness, today too the matter is possible; and therefore everyone can realise his ideals and merit to live truly."}]},{"slug":"11-02-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-02-18/","title_he":"יח – יציאה מן הארץ לצורך נישואין","title_en":"18. Leaving the Land for the Sake of Marriage","body_en":"<p>The value of marriage is so great that the Sages permitted a young man or young woman to leave the Land of Israel for abroad in order to find their match. Of course, ideally it is preferable that they find their match among the sons or daughters of the Land and not need to leave the Land for this; but if it seems to them that they will not be able to find their match in the Land, they may go abroad in order to marry, on condition that afterwards they return to the Land.</p>\n<p>And although going out for the sake of a wedding may stretch over a long period – for even after a meeting is arranged, sometimes the young man is not interested and sometimes the young woman is not interested, and sometimes these clarifications take a long time; and also from the time he finds his partner until they marry time passes, and it is not always possible to make aliya immediately after the wedding – despite this the Sages permitted going abroad because of the great importance of the mitzva of marriage.</p>\n<p>But to go abroad and marry there a woman who is not willing to make aliya to the Land, with the intention that the groom too will remain with her in exile, is forbidden; for all that they permitted leaving the Land in order to marry a wife is only for a temporary departure, of months or a number of years, but a permanent departure they did not permit (Rambam, Hilkhot Melakhim 5:9).</p>\n<p>And all this is specifically from the Land of Israel to abroad, for it is forbidden to leave the Land permanently. But between two countries abroad, or between two regions in the Land, the groom may stay and live in his wife's country and not live near his parents. Of course, if the matter is necessary for the parents, it is preferable to fulfil their wish and live near them; but if the bride holds to her view and wants to remain in her place, the groom may set aside the mitzva of honouring parents in order to marry a wife (Responsa of the Rashba attributed to the Ramban, siman 272; and see SA YD 239:8 and the Shakh there; above 1:11).</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
